@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex Tessera
  Tessera changes how Gnus summaries, mu4e headers, and Elfeed search
results are displayed in Emacs.  It combines subjects, contacts, dates,
labels, and state glyphs in layouts that share spacing and highlighting
rules.  You continue to use Gnus, mu4e, or Elfeed commands for
searching, marking, reading, navigating, and folding threads.

  An @dfn{adapter} is the Tessera integration for one of these
applications.  In this manual, @dfn{native} refers to the application's
own commands, settings, and message information.

  This manual first explains how to enable and customize the existing
adapters.  Later chapters describe the layout engine for Lisp programs
that supply their own entries.  Knowledge of ordinary Emacs commands and
the selected mail or feed application is assumed.

@menu
* Package Boundaries::  Core and experimental packages.
* Display Concepts::    Entries, segments, glyphs, and slots.
@end menu

@node Package Boundaries
@section Package Boundaries

  The @code{tessera} package contains the common engine and three native
adapters.  Requiring @code{tessera-gnus}, @code{tessera-mu4e}, or
@code{tessera-elfeed} exposes that adapter's public settings without
enabling it.  The corresponding global minor mode activates its view.

  Packages named @code{tessera-x} and @samp{tessera-x-*} provide
experimental features.  Their @samp{x} means experimental.  The current
features include context snapshots: buffers containing selected message
or feed text for inspection or use by another application.  Preparing
these snapshots does not require a Tessera display mode.  For them, see
@ref{Top,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}, @ref{Top,,, tessera-x-gnus, Tessera X
Gnus}, @ref{Top,,, tessera-x-mu4e, Tessera X mu4e}, and @ref{Top,,,
tessera-x-elfeed, Tessera X Elfeed}.

@node Display Concepts
@section Display Concepts
@cindex entries
@cindex segments
@cindex glyph slots
  An @dfn{entry} is one native record, such as a message header or feed
item.  It occupies one logical buffer line even when the layout shows
two visual lines.  A @dfn{segment} is a piece of text, such as the
subject.  A @dfn{glyph} is a state symbol with alternative visual forms.
A @dfn{glyph slot} reserves a fixed width and selects at most one glyph
variant for an entry.

  Padding and alignment affect the display without adding blank message
rows.  Native commands still select and operate on the underlying
message or feed entry.

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

  Install the core package and the native application you use, then
enable its adapter.  You can enable the adapters independently.

@menu
* Requirements::         Required packages and loading libraries.
* Enabling Adapters::    Enabling modes and restoring native views.
* Customization Scope::  Shared and buffer-local settings.
@end menu

@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex installation
  Tessera 0.1.0 declares dependencies on Emacs 30.1 or later and Alert
1.2 or later.  Gnus is bundled with Emacs.  Install mu4e or Elfeed
separately when using those adapters.  They are optional native
backends, not requirements for loading the common engine.

  For a source checkout, put @file{lisp/tessera/} on @code{load-path};
adding only @file{lisp/} is insufficient.  Replace the path below with
your checkout:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/emacs-tessera/lisp/tessera/")
@end lisp

  A package installation normally arranges @code{load-path} and
autoloads.

@node Enabling Adapters
@section Enabling and Disabling Adapters

  Load the library and enable its mode in your initialization file.  For
example:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-gnus)
(tessera-gnus-mode 1)
@end lisp

@noindent
Use @code{tessera-mu4e} and @code{tessera-mu4e-mode}, or
@code{tessera-elfeed} and @code{tessera-elfeed-mode}, in the
corresponding configuration for another backend.  This does not start
Gnus, mu4e, or Elfeed, or configure accounts and feeds.

  The modes apply to existing matching buffers and install hooks for
future ones.  Each adapter initially selects @code{two-line} locally.
Native Gnus or mu4e threading can then select a thread layout instead.
@xref{Choosing a Layout}.

@cindex restoring native settings
  Invoke the mode command again to toggle it interactively, or call it
with @code{-1} in Lisp.  Disabling restores the native settings saved at
activation, removes the adapter's hooks and decorations, and restores
the native presentation.  A previously local setting is restored as a
local setting; an inherited setting returns to its default binding.

  Tessera temporarily replaces whole-line highlighting with its own
entry highlight in affected buffers.  The prior @code{hl-line-mode}
state is restored when the adapter is disabled.  Message marks and read
state are retained.

@node Customization Scope
@section Customization Scope
@cindex customization
  Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} tessera @key{RET}} for shared
options.  The backend groups are @code{tessera-gnus},
@code{tessera-mu4e}, and @code{tessera-elfeed}.  Faces are
theme-sensitive and can be customized through the usual Emacs face
interface.

  Shared layout options are ordinary user options; a buffer-local
binding can specialize one view.  Set a local layout after the adapter
has activated, because activation installs its initial local layout.  An
already rendered Elfeed buffer needs an update to reflect shared option
changes; Gnus and mu4e track the appearance settings during their normal
refresh cycle.  @xref{Refreshing the Display}.

@node Entry Layouts
@chapter Entry Layouts

  The built-in flat layouts arrange the same native records in one or
two visual lines.  They do not change result membership or read state.

@menu
* Choosing a Layout::   Selecting one-line or two-line entries.
* Entry Spacing::       Margins, gaps, and padding.
* Narrow Windows::      Shrinking or omitting entry fields.
* Entry Highlighting: Highlighting and Hover.  Highlighting point and mouse targets.
@end menu

@node Choosing a Layout
@section Choosing a Layout
@cindex layout selection
@defopt tessera-entry-layout
The symbolic identifier of the ordinary entry layout.  Its global
initial value is @code{single-line}; built-in adapters also register
@code{two-line}.  Each adapter sets a local value of @code{two-line}
when first enabled in a buffer.  An unregistered layout name is an error
when that backend renders an ordinary entry.
@end defopt

  For an already active buffer, evaluate the following with @kbd{M-:} to
select the compact flat layout:

@lisp
(setq-local tessera-entry-layout 'single-line)
@end lisp

  Gnus and mu4e use their thread layout whenever their native threading
is enabled, regardless of this option.  Turn native threading off when
choosing a flat layout.  There is no additional Tessera thread toggle.

@node Entry Spacing
@section Entry Spacing
@cindex padding, horizontal
  Entry spacing controls the margins, separation between fields, and
padding above and below the content.  Horizontal and vertical dimensions
use different units.

  Horizontal dimensions are measured in character columns.  They are
nonnegative integers.  The outside gap separates the entry surface from
the window edge; inner padding belongs inside the surface.

@defopt tessera-entry-safe-gap
Outside gap on each side of an entry, initially @code{1}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-entry-left-padding
Padding inside the left edge, initially @code{1}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-entry-right-padding
Padding inside the right edge, initially @code{1}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-entry-segment-gap
Gap between adjacent visible text segments, initially @code{1}.  It also
separates leading slots from text and affects thread indentation.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-entry-flex-gap-min-width
Minimum separation between left and right content, initially @code{1}.
Right content is aligned to the window edge within the right padding and
outside gap.
@end defopt

@cindex padding, vertical
  Vertical padding is measured in normal line heights and accepts
nonnegative numbers, including fractions.  It is displayed as decoration
rather than inserted blank logical lines.

@defopt tessera-entry-top-padding
Height above an ordinary entry, initially @code{0.2}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-entry-bottom-padding
Height below an ordinary entry, initially @code{0.2}.
@end defopt

  Thread entries use separate outer and inner padding values.
@xref{Thread Spacing}.

@node Narrow Windows
@section Narrow Windows
@cindex truncation
  Layouts assign different width policies to their fields.  Subjects and
other flexible fields can shrink; optional labels or contacts can be
omitted; dates and fixed glyph channels retain their roles.  Hover help
can retain information that the visible text abbreviates.

  The renderer does not guarantee that arbitrary fixed fields fit an
arbitrarily small window.  Width policies are described for extension
authors in @ref{Segment Widths}.  Deep native threads have an additional
clipping rule that preserves the positions of the visible tree columns.
@xref{Deep Threads}.

@node Highlighting and Hover
@section Highlighting and Hover
@cindex point highlight
@cindex mouse hover
@defvr Face tessera-entry-current-face
The face for the entry containing point.  It inherits @code{hl-line}
without extending its face role across unrelated content.
@end defvr

@defvr Face tessera-entry-hover-face
The shared hover face, inheriting @code{highlight}.
@end defvr

  The point highlight preserves native content faces and interactions.
For a row that also displays a thread heading, it applies only to the
message line, leaving the heading in its ordinary faces.  In an ordinary
two-line entry, both content lines are highlighted.

  Hover is per element.  Decorative gaps, margins, empty slots,
alignment padding, and blank tree indentation do not acquire a hover
highlight.  Spaces within a subject or contact remain part of that
content's hover range.  Semantic glyph foregrounds are retained on
hover.

@node Glyph Appearance
@chapter Glyph Appearance

  Glyphs provide compact state information.  Their meaning is
independent of the visual style and of the theme's particular colors.

@menu
* Glyph Styles::        ASCII, Unicode, and Nerd Icons forms.
* Glyph Colors::        Color settings and semantic faces.
* Glyph Widths::        Fixed slots and font scaling.
@end menu

@node Glyph Styles
@section Glyph Styles

@defopt tessera-glyph-style
Preferred glyph style, initially @code{unicode}.  The allowed values are
@code{ascii}, @code{unicode}, and @code{nerd-icons}.
@end defopt

  @code{ascii} uses each glyph's ASCII form.  @code{unicode} uses its
Unicode form on a graphical frame when all characters can be displayed,
and otherwise falls back to ASCII.  @code{nerd-icons} first tries the
optional Nerd Icons library and suitable glyph, then Unicode, then
ASCII.  Install the optional library and fonts separately to use it.
Terminal frames use the ASCII fallback for these glyph objects.

  Thread connectors have their own simple choice: ASCII connectors when
the option is @code{ascii}, Unicode connectors otherwise.

@node Glyph Colors
@section Glyph Colors

@defopt tessera-glyph-color
Color treatment, initially @code{t}.  A @code{t} value uses semantic
faces; @code{nil} inherits the surrounding foreground; a color string
sets a uniform foreground for every glyph.
@end defopt

  Backend glyph faces can override the shared semantic face when this
option is @code{t}.  Monochrome and uniform-color settings retain their
meaning even when a variant supplies such an override.

@cindex semantic faces
  The following faces inherit theme roles.  Tessera does not assign a
literal palette or special-case theme names.  Two roles may have the
same color when their parent faces do.

@defvar tessera-glyph-semantics
The recognized roles: @code{accent}, @code{attention},
@code{informational}, @code{muted}, @code{negative}, @code{neutral},
@code{positive}, and @code{warning}.  This describes the renderer's
vocabulary, not a user option for installing a new palette.
@end defvar

@vtable @code
@item tessera-glyph-accent-face
New or unread states and distinctive attributes.  Inherits
@code{font-lock-keyword-face} with bold weight.

@item tessera-glyph-attention-face
Important or pending actions.  Inherits @code{warning} with bold weight.

@item tessera-glyph-informational-face
Attributes such as attachments, signatures, and forwarded messages.
Inherits @code{font-lock-type-face}.

@item tessera-glyph-muted-face
Read, inactive, and low-priority states.  Inherits @code{shadow}.

@item tessera-glyph-negative-face
Failures and destructive actions.  Inherits @code{error}.

@item tessera-glyph-neutral-face
An unaccented glyph.  Inherits @code{default}.

@item tessera-glyph-positive-face
Completed actions and available content.  Inherits @code{success}.

@item tessera-glyph-warning-face
Urgent or uncertain states.  Inherits @code{warning}.
@end vtable

  A signature or encryption glyph indicates content presence, not
successful verification or decryption.  Pending actions are distinct
from already completed actions.

@node Glyph Widths
@section Glyph Widths

  Registered glyph slots have fixed column widths.  Empty slots and
alignment padding preserve that capacity.  On graphical frames, font
glyphs that exceed the pixel budget are scaled down; smaller ones retain
their size and are aligned within the slot.  Oversized forms can still
signal an error if they cannot fit.

  Built-in mail layouts pack their visible prefix icons toward the right
edge of the available prefix area.  Inline content icons have
independent optional slots and do not enlarge that prefix area.

@node Native Threads
@chapter Native Threads
@cindex threading
  Tessera displays the threads in the current Gnus or mu4e results,
using the application's ordering and reply relationships.  Messages
absent from those results are not fetched to complete the display.
Preparing snapshots of a message and its replies is a separate
experimental feature.

@menu
* Thread Headings::     Subjects, representatives, and counts.
* Native Folding::      Using the mail reader's thread commands.
* Thread Spacing::      Padding around and within threads.
* Deep Threads::        Clipping long branches in narrow windows.
@end menu

@node Thread Headings
@section Thread Headings

  With native threading enabled, one message in each thread carries a
heading containing unread/total counts and the thread subject.  This
message is called the @dfn{representative}; its own information follows
the heading.  Other members use one visual line and omit the subject.
The representative need not be the original message when that message is
absent from the current results.

  Counts include folded members of the current result set.  They do not
claim to count every message on a server.  Heading faces use aggregate
unread state; each message retains its own read state for its dates and
other per-message elements.

@node Native Folding
@section Native Folding
@cindex folding
  Use the application's existing thread and folding commands.
@code{gnus-show-threads} and @code{mu4e-search-threads} are the switches
that select the thread layout in their respective adapters.  Tessera
reuses native ordering, parentage, filtering, and fold visibility.

  For mu4e, the native folded-message summary remains present.  Tessera
adjusts spacing at the fold boundary and includes hidden members in
counts.  A folded row does not receive separate visible padding.

@node Thread Spacing
@section Thread Spacing
@cindex thread padding
  These options use nonnegative normal line heights.  They replace the
ordinary entry top and bottom values for entries displayed in a thread
layout.

@defopt tessera-thread-outer-top-padding
Space above the representative, initially @code{0.2}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-thread-outer-bottom-padding
Space below the last visible member, initially @code{0.2}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-thread-inner-top-padding
Space above other members, initially @code{0.05}.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-thread-inner-bottom-padding
Space below members that are not the last visible member, initially
@code{0.05}.
@end defopt

  A completely folded thread uses outer boundaries around its remaining
visible representation.  Spacing does not insert an extra divider.

@node Deep Threads
@section Deep Threads

  Branch columns keep their native depth even in a narrow window.
Tessera clips the right edge of overflowing left content while reserving
space for right metadata.  An ellipsis indicates the hidden remainder;
its hover help includes the contact, subject, depth, and parent
identity.

  If the usual position for point is hidden, navigating to the entry
places point on the ellipsis.  Widening the window restores that
position in the message text.  Indentation continues to show the same
reply relationships.

@node Gnus
@chapter Gnus

  The Gnus adapter renders Summary entries and observes already prepared
Article MIME information.  Gnus continues to own article retrieval and
all ordinary article operations.

@menu
* Gnus Mode::           Enabling the Gnus adapter.
* Gnus Row Contents::   Subjects, contacts, dates, and labels.
* Gnus Marks::          Article state, availability, and scores.
* Content Indicators: Gnus Content Indicators.  Attachments, signatures, and encryption.
* Gnus Faces::          Text styling and glyph faces.
* Gnus Navigation::     Moving through articles and refreshing rows.
@end menu

@node Gnus Mode
@section Enabling Gnus Presentation

@deffn Command tessera-gnus-mode &optional arg
Toggle the global Gnus adapter.  From Lisp, an omitted or @code{nil}
argument enables it, as does a positive number; a nonpositive number
disables it.  The symbol @code{toggle} toggles it.  Interactively, no
prefix toggles it.  Configure existing and future Summary buffers and
track prepared Article information while enabled.
@end deffn
@vindex tessera-gnus-mode-hook
  The adapter saves @code{gnus-summary-line-format}, installs its native
user-format entry, sets the local layout to @code{two-line}, and
suspends local @code{hl-line-mode}.  Disabling restores the saved
settings and native Summary rows.  Public faces and options remain
available after disabling.

@node Gnus Row Contents
@section Gnus Row Contents

  With @code{gnus-show-threads} nil, a single-line row has the score,
availability, secondary, and status prefix, followed by subject and
available content icons.  Optional labels and author, then date, occupy
the right side.

  The two-line layout puts secondary and status icons beside the
subject, with labels on the right.  Its second line contains score and
availability icons, the author and content icons, and the date.  The
initial local layout is two-line.

@cindex labels, Gnus
  Labels combine enabled registry marks, @samp{X-GM-LABELS}, and
@samp{Keywords} fields available in the header.  Equal label names are
shown once; hover help records their sources and the full label list.
Separators are neutral.  Missing extra headers do not cause Tessera to
retrieve a message solely to discover labels.

  Contact text uses names without recipient prefixes or address
suffixes, falling back to addresses when names are absent.  Native
recipient selection remains effective; a news article without a
recipient shows its author.  Hover help retains full From and available
To fields.

@node Gnus Marks
@section Gnus Marks and Availability
@cindex Gnus marks
  Four prefix channels reflect the native summary mark characters:
status, secondary state, download availability, and score.  They retain
Gnus's own meaning and classification.  A recognized native mark selects
a corresponding glyph; the no-mark value leaves an empty channel; an
unfamiliar mark is shown as unknown rather than rewritten.

  Status covers unread, ticked, dormant, expirable, spam, and the other
native read or removal states.  Secondary state includes process, cache,
reply, forward, save, and unseen marks.  Availability distinguishes
downloaded from undownloaded articles.  Score indicates values above or
below the native threshold.  Glyph selection does not change these
marks.

@node Gnus Content Indicators
@section Gnus Content Indicators
@cindex MIME observations
  Attachment indicators become available after Gnus has examined the
article's MIME parts.  Signature and encryption indicators can also use
hints in the Content-Type header.  An absent icon can mean that the
content was not found or that it has not yet been examined.

  Tessera does not verify or decrypt mail.  After Gnus processes
security parts, hover help can include the results; a processing error
uses the error face.  The availability of a result does not itself mean
that verification succeeded.  Encrypted content can still contain
attachments or signatures that have not been examined.

@node Gnus Faces
@section Gnus Faces
@cindex faces, Gnus
  In flat layouts, subjects retain the face chosen by Gnus's native
summary highlighting rules, with extra bold emphasis for unread
articles.  Author and date styling depends on the article's own native
read classification, independently of special score or mark colors.
Authors are italic.

  In thread layouts, the subject uses aggregate unread state and remains
bold.  Each author instead carries its article's complete native state,
with italics and unread bold emphasis.  Dates still use the individual
article's read state.  Spam and expirable faces supplement native state
on ordinary subjects and thread authors.

  The following faces belong to @code{tessera-gnus-summary}.  They
customize the text and glyph roles used in Summary rows.

@vtable @code
@item tessera-gnus-summary-subject-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-unread-subject-face
Subject adjustment over the native state; unread emphasis inherits
@code{bold}.  The base subject inherits @code{gnus-header-subject}.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-author-face
Thread author base, inheriting @code{italic} and
@code{gnus-header-from}, with normal base weight.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-read-author-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-unread-author-face
Flat authors, based on @code{gnus-summary-normal-read} or
@code{gnus-summary-normal-unread}; both are italic and unread is bold.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-thread-subject-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-thread-unread-subject-face
Bold headings based on native normal-read or normal-unread faces,
selected from the whole thread's unread count.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-date-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-unread-date-face
Upright dates based on native normal-read or normal-unread faces.  Read
dates have normal weight; unread dates are bold.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-label-face
Labels based on @code{gnus-header-content}, with normal weight and
slant.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-status-face
Status glyphs inheriting the shared accent face.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-muted-face
Read and inactive glyphs inheriting the shared muted face.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-important-face
Ticked and pending-processing glyphs inheriting attention.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-positive-face
Availability and completed-action glyphs inheriting positive.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-informational-face
Informational states inheriting informational.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-warning-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-error-face
Uncertain or failed states inheriting the shared warning or negative
face, respectively.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-spam-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-expirable-face
Spam and expirable article states inheriting @code{error} and
@code{warning}, respectively.  These also style their status icons.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-high-score-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-low-score-face
Score glyphs inheriting attention or muted, respectively.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-attachment-face
@itemx tessera-gnus-summary-signature-face
Content-presence glyphs inheriting informational.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-encryption-face
Encryption-presence glyphs inheriting accent.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-thread-tree-face
Thread branches inheriting @code{shadow}.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-thread-count-face
Counts inheriting @code{gnus-summary-normal-read} and @code{shadow}.

@item tessera-gnus-summary-thread-unread-count-face
Counts inheriting @code{gnus-summary-normal-unread} and @code{bold}.
@end vtable

@node Gnus Navigation
@section Gnus Navigation and Refresh

  Moving to an article places point on its subject in flat layouts and
its author in thread layouts.  Updates to marks or row contents preserve
point's position within the article as far as the new text allows.

  While Tessera is active, Gnus's automatic horizontal recentering is
suppressed because a virtual heading and its message share a logical
line.  Native vertical recentering retains
@code{gnus-auto-center-summary}, including a numeric target row.  Manual
horizontal scrolling remains available.  Disabling Tessera restores
native horizontal recentering.

  Summary updates, folds, appearance changes, and window resizing update
the affected rows.  The adapter evaluates native score, cache, and
read-state rules rather than maintaining its own parallel rule set.

@node mu4e
@chapter mu4e

  The mu4e adapter renders Headers rows and adjusts native fold spacing.
The current query, pending marks, document identities, and native
navigation continue to belong to mu4e.

@menu
* mu4e Mode::           Enabling the mu4e adapter.
* mu4e Row Contents::   Message fields, targets, and labels.
* State Precedence: mu4e State Precedence.  Choosing among competing message states.
* mu4e Faces::          Text styling and glyph faces.
@end menu

@node mu4e Mode
@section Enabling mu4e Presentation

@deffn Command tessera-mu4e-mode &optional arg
Toggle the global mu4e Headers adapter, with the usual global minor mode
argument convention described for @code{tessera-gnus-mode}.  Apply it to
existing Headers buffers and future Headers mode buffers.
@end deffn
@vindex tessera-mu4e-mode-hook
  The initial local layout is @code{two-line}.  While active, Tessera
hides the native column header and replaces whole-line highlighting.
Disabling restores native rows, pending-mark presentation, header line,
highlighting state, and saved local settings.

@node mu4e Row Contents
@section mu4e Row Contents

  With @code{mu4e-search-threads} nil, the single-line prefix is
operation, priority, secondary state, and status.  Subject, optional
operation target, and content icons follow it; labels, an optional
contact, and date occupy the right side.

  In two-line layout, operation and status accompany the subject and
optional target, with labels on the right.  Priority and secondary state
accompany the contact and content icons on the second line, with date on
the right.

  With threading enabled, the representative receives a count and
subject heading; each message line contains its own contact, content,
labels, and date.  The prefix order is priority, operation, secondary,
status.

@cindex pending operations, mu4e
  When @code{mu4e-headers-show-target} is non-@code{nil}, an available
pending operation target appears beside the subject in flat layouts, or
after the contact's content icons in thread layouts.  A pending trash or
delete action uses a destructive-operation face; it does not claim that
the action has already completed.

  Labels and tags are merged without duplicates.  Each label's help
identifies whether it came from labels, tags, or both.  Separators
retain a neutral face.

  Contacts show names with address fallback and retain native recipient
selection for outgoing mail.  Hover help retains full contact data.
Dates use native mu4e human-date formatting.

@node mu4e State Precedence
@section mu4e State Precedence
@cindex state precedence, mu4e
  Each prefix slot selects one eligible state.  Earlier states in the
following list take precedence.  Hover help also lists active eligible
states that the selected glyph hides.

@table @code
@item operation
The pending native mark, including its target when present.

@item priority
High priority, flagged, then low priority.

@item secondary
Replied, forwarded, personal, then mailing-list membership.

@item status
Trashed, draft, new, unread, then read.
@end table

  Optional flags follow @code{mu4e-headers-visible-flags} both in glyph
selection and help.  Hiding one can expose the next eligible state.
Read-state calculations and thread counts still use native unread/new
membership even when another status glyph takes precedence.

  Each prefix slot is two columns wide.  Four slots occupy eight
columns; the two-line layout allocates two slots per line.  Thread
counts can enlarge the shared leading area.  Attachment, signature,
encryption, and calendar indicators use independent optional content
slots.

@node mu4e Faces
@section mu4e Faces
@cindex faces, mu4e
  Ordinary subjects preserve mu4e's native message-state styling,
including strike-through or slant, with bold emphasis for unread/new
messages.  Flat contacts and dates depend only on read state.  Contacts
are italic; dates are upright.  Missing unread foreground attributes
fall back to @code{default}, rather than borrowing the read face's
foreground.

  Thread subjects are bold and depend on aggregate unread state.  Thread
contacts preserve each message's native state with italic styling and
unread/new bold emphasis.  Dates remain per-message.  Labels and content
attributes are not aggregated across a conversation.

  The following faces belong to @code{tessera-mu4e-headers}.  Semantic
icon roles use the shared faces from @ref{Glyph Colors}.

@vtable @code
@item tessera-mu4e-headers-subject-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-unread-subject-face
Ordinary subject adjustments over native message state; the unread
variant adds bold emphasis without a fallback foreground.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-read-contact-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-unread-contact-face
Flat contacts based on @code{mu4e-header-face} or
@code{mu4e-unread-face}, with @code{default} fallback.  Both are italic;
read is normal weight and unread is bold.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-subject-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-unread-subject-face
Bold, upright headings based on @code{mu4e-header-face} or
@code{mu4e-unread-face}, with @code{default} fallback.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-contact-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-unread-contact-face
Italic adjustments over each message's native state.  The unread variant
adds bold weight.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-tree-face
Thread branches inheriting @code{mu4e-header-face}.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-count-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-thread-unread-count-face
Counts based on @code{mu4e-header-face} or @code{mu4e-unread-face}, with
@code{default} fallback and bold unread emphasis.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-date-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-unread-date-face
Upright read or unread dates, using the corresponding native face and
@code{default} fallback; unread dates are bold.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-label-face
Labels inheriting @code{mu4e-header-value-face}, with normal weight and
slant.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-operation-face
Pending operations inheriting the shared attention face.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-destructive-operation-face
Pending trash or deletion, adding the negative face over operation.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-new-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-unread-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-personal-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-encryption-face
New, unread, personal, or encrypted-content glyphs inheriting accent.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-read-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-low-priority-face
Read and low-priority glyphs inheriting muted.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-draft-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-forwarded-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-list-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-attachment-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-signature-face
Draft, correspondence, and content-presence glyphs inheriting
informational.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-trashed-face
An existing trashed state, inheriting negative.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-flagged-face
@itemx tessera-mu4e-headers-calendar-face
Flagged messages and calendar content, inheriting attention.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-replied-face
Replied messages, inheriting positive.

@item tessera-mu4e-headers-high-priority-face
High-priority messages, inheriting warning.
@end vtable

@node Elfeed
@chapter Elfeed

  The Elfeed adapter changes Search rows while retaining native entry,
feed, tag, and date interactions.  It does not update feeds merely to
render a row.

@menu
* Elfeed Mode::           Enabling the Elfeed adapter.
* Elfeed Row Contents::   Titles, feeds, links, tags, and dates.
* Elfeed Glyph Options::  Customizing status and enclosure glyphs.
* Elfeed Faces::          Styling read and unread entries.
@end menu

@node Elfeed Mode
@section Enabling Elfeed Presentation

@deffn Command tessera-elfeed-mode &optional arg
Toggle the global Elfeed Search adapter, with the usual minor mode
argument convention described for @code{tessera-gnus-mode}.  Configure
existing and future Search buffers.
@end deffn
@vindex tessera-elfeed-mode-hook
  Activation saves the native print function, layout, separator date
format, and highlighting state.  It installs the Tessera print function,
selects local @code{two-line} layout, and sets the separator date format
to @code{nil}.  Disabling restores those settings and native rendering.
Existing native separator strings are kept in the default font rather
than inheriting an adjacent icon font.

@node Elfeed Row Contents
@section Elfeed Row Contents

  The single-line layout shows read/unread status, title, and date.  The
two-line layout puts status and title on its first line, with feed title
on the right.  The second line contains the entry URL, an optional
enclosure indicator, tags, and date.  Reserved status space aligns the
second line's content.

  The native title override is respected, with the entry URL as a
fallback when the title is empty.  The @code{unread} tag controls read
state and is omitted from the textual tag list.  Other native tag faces
remain part of title styling.  Tag punctuation remains neutral.

  The enclosure indicator describes available enclosure media types on
hover; it does not download enclosures.

@node Elfeed Glyph Options
@section Elfeed Glyph Options
@cindex glyph customization, Elfeed
  The following values are five-element lists containing ASCII text,
Unicode text, Nerd Icons function, Nerd Icons name, and semantic role.
Set them through Customize or @code{setopt} so their custom setter
re-registers the glyphs and refreshes active Search buffers.

@defopt tessera-elfeed-search-unread-glyph
The unread glyph.  Initially:
@lisp
("*" "●" nerd-icons-mdicon "nf-md-email" accent)
@end lisp
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-elfeed-search-read-glyph
The read glyph.  Initially:
@lisp
("o" "○" nerd-icons-mdicon "nf-md-email_open_outline" muted)
@end lisp
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-elfeed-search-enclosure-glyph
The enclosure glyph.  Initially:
@lisp
("@@" "📎" nerd-icons-mdicon "nf-md-paperclip" informational)
@end lisp
@end defopt

  The options belong to @code{tessera-elfeed}.  The read and unread
glyphs must fit the status slot's two-column width.  For example, to
change the unread Unicode form while retaining its semantic role:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-elfeed)
(setopt tessera-elfeed-search-unread-glyph
        '("*" "•" nerd-icons-mdicon "nf-md-email" accent))
@end lisp

@node Elfeed Faces
@section Elfeed Faces
@cindex faces, Elfeed
  Title faces follow native Elfeed title and unread-title roles; other
native tag-driven title faces remain effective.  Feed names and URLs are
italic, while dates are upright.  Unread feed names, dates, and URLs
receive unread emphasis.

@vtable @code
@item tessera-elfeed-search-title-face
@itemx tessera-elfeed-search-unread-title-face
Entry titles inheriting the corresponding native Elfeed title faces.

@item tessera-elfeed-search-feed-face
@itemx tessera-elfeed-search-unread-feed-face
Feed titles inheriting @code{elfeed-search-feed-face} with italic slant
and normal read weight.  The unread variant adds bold.

@item tessera-elfeed-search-tag-face
Tags inheriting @code{elfeed-search-tag-face}.

@item tessera-elfeed-search-date-face
Read dates using native title and date faces, with normal weight and
slant.

@item tessera-elfeed-search-unread-date-face
Unread dates using the unread title color and bold weight, with upright
slant.

@item tessera-elfeed-search-url-face
@itemx tessera-elfeed-search-unread-url-face
URLs using the corresponding date color, with italic slant and no
underline.  The unread variant retains unread emphasis.
@end vtable

@node Refreshing the Display
@chapter Refreshing the Display
@cindex refreshing
  Gnus and mu4e track native changes, theme changes, and shared
appearance options during their normal update cycles.  After manually
changing individual faces, regenerate the affected native view to
refresh any resolved hover colors as well as text faces.

  In an active Elfeed Search buffer, force a local display update after
changing a shared layout option:

@lisp
(elfeed-search-update :force)
@end lisp

  This redraws the search display; it is not a feed download.  Elfeed's
three glyph options have custom setters that also refresh active
buffers.  Assigning them with @code{setq} bypasses that setter, so
prefer Customize or @code{setopt}.

@node Writing an Entry Backend
@chapter Writing an Entry Backend

  This chapter is for Lisp programmers adding a display adapter.  Your
adapter supplies records, interprets their state, composes faces, and
refreshes the view.  Tessera validates layouts, allocates widths,
renders glyphs, and supplies spacing and navigation positions.

  Register a backend, render its records into the intended buffer, and
attach each entry's layout after inserting the native terminating
newline.  Keep source navigation and lifecycle behavior in the adapter.
Private names containing @samp{--} are not supported extension entry
points.

@menu
* Entry Contexts::       The data used to render one entry.
* Registration: Registering a Backend.  Registering providers and layouts.
* Segment Providers::    Supplying text and its properties.
* Segment Widths::       Truncation, priorities, and optional fields.
* Layout Objects::       Placing fields on the content lines.
* Glyph Definitions::    Defining and rendering state symbols.
* Glyph Slots::          Selecting glyphs and reserving their space.
* Entry Insertion: Rendering and Insertion.  Inserting rows and attaching decorations.
* Navigation Anchors::   Preserving point across display updates.
* Highlight Lifecycle::  Installing and removing entry highlights.
* Backend Example::      A small working display adapter.
@end menu

@node Entry Contexts
@section Entry Contexts
@cindex entry contexts
@deftp {Data Type} tessera-entry-context
The environment for rendering one native record.  Construct it with
@code{make-tessera-entry-context} and keyword arguments for the fields
below.  Accessors have the form
@samp{tessera-entry-context-@var{field}}.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-entry-context
@findex tessera-entry-context-p
@findex tessera-entry-context-backend
@findex tessera-entry-context-object
@findex tessera-entry-context-buffer
@findex tessera-entry-context-window
@findex tessera-entry-context-metadata
@findex tessera-entry-context-thread
@table @code
@item backend
The registered backend symbol.  It must match the renderer's backend.

@item object
The native record, opaque to the engine.

@item buffer
The buffer in which rendering occurs.

@item window
The target window, or @code{nil} when the buffer has no display window.

@item metadata
Additional adapter-owned data, opaque to the engine.

@item thread
A @code{tessera-thread-context}, or @code{nil} for an ordinary entry.
@end table

  An entry context is not a @code{tessera-x-context} snapshot request.
It holds the environment for rendering one row, not extracted source
material or asynchronous work.

@node Registering a Backend
@section Registering a Backend
@cindex backend registration
@defun tessera-entry-register backend &key context segments @
  glyph-slots layouts thread-layout
Register or replace the definition for the non-@code{nil} symbol
@var{backend}, returning that symbol.  Validate the new definition
before installing it; invalid definitions signal an error without
replacing the previous registration.
@end defun

  @var{context} is a function called with the native object, current
buffer, and target window.  It must return a matching entry context.
@var{segments} is an alist from unique segment symbols to functions of
one entry context.  @var{glyph-slots} is a list of uniquely named
@code{tessera-glyph-slot} objects.

  @var{layouts} is a nonempty alist from unique layout symbols to
@code{tessera-entry-layout} objects.  @var{thread-layout} is optional;
when present it contains ordinary head and child entry layouts.  All
references must name registered segments or slots, and property lists
must use the supported keys and value types.

  Registration does not enable a mode, create a buffer, or refresh an
existing view.  The adapter must arrange these operations.  Its context
provider and segment providers should read native state without changing
message identity, marks, or source content.

@node Segment Providers
@section Segment Providers

  A segment provider receives one entry context and returns a string or
@code{nil}.  @code{nil} omits the segment.  A string may contain faces,
help, keymaps, and other native interaction properties, but it must not
contain newline or carriage-return characters.  The engine signals an
error for multiline text or a non-string non-@code{nil} result.

  Use a bare segment symbol in a layout for its natural width.  To give
it a width policy, use a list whose first element is the symbol and
whose remaining elements form a property list.  For example:

@lisp
(subject :grow t :min-width 4 :truncate tail :point t)
@end lisp

@noindent
Do not insert alignment spaces into the returned text.  Supply content
and properties; let the layout allocate decoration around it.

@node Segment Widths
@section Segment Widths
@cindex width allocation
  Segment references accept the following keys.  Unspecified flags are
@code{nil}; the default minimum and priority are zero.

@table @code
@item :grow
A boolean marking a flexible segment.  When truncation is allowed, these
segments shrink before other truncatable fields.  Freed space can
restore their text up to natural width; this is not blank filling.

@item :min-width
A nonnegative integer limiting shrinkage.  It does not pad short text to
that size.

@item :max-width
An optional nonnegative integer capping text when @code{:truncate} is
non-@code{nil}.  It must not be smaller than an explicit minimum.  A cap
without a truncation method does not cut text.

@item :truncate
@code{head}, @code{middle}, or @code{tail} removes text at the named
location when needed.  @code{nil} keeps the text intact.

@item :priority
An integer, initially zero.  Lower-priority fields shrink or disappear
first; higher-priority flexible fields regain available space first.
Equal priorities retain the order of the fields.

@item :optional
A boolean allowing the entire segment to be removed when space remains
insufficient after shrinking flexible fields.

@item :point
A boolean marking the first displayed character as a preferred
navigation anchor.  @xref{Navigation Anchors}.
@end table

  Allocation first shrinks flexible truncatable fields, then removes
optional fields from lower to higher priority.  If omission frees space,
flexible text can regain it.  Remaining overflow is handled by shrinking
other truncatable fields.  Fixed untruncatable text is not silently
discarded by these policies.

@node Layout Objects
@section Layout Objects

@deftp {Data Type} tessera-entry-layout
Placement of leading content and left and right segments.  Construct
with @code{make-tessera-entry-layout} and field keywords.  The fields
below default to @code{nil}.  Their accessors use the prefix
@samp{tessera-entry-layout-}.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-entry-layout
@findex tessera-entry-layout-p
@findex tessera-entry-layout-main-glyph-slots
@findex tessera-entry-layout-main-left-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-main-right-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-extra-glyph-slots
@findex tessera-entry-layout-extra-left-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-extra-right-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-glyph-slots-align
@findex tessera-entry-layout-main-leading-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-extra-leading-segments
@findex tessera-entry-layout-leading-width
@table @code
@item main-glyph-slots
Slot references for the main line's prefix.

@item main-left-segments
@itemx main-right-segments
Segment references for the main line's left and right content.

@item extra-glyph-slots
@itemx extra-left-segments
@itemx extra-right-segments
Corresponding references for a second visual line.

@item glyph-slots-align
@code{nil} preserves individual prefix slot positions.  @code{left} or
@code{right} packs visible prefix icons toward that edge within the
slots' capacity.  Inline slot groups are unaffected.

@item main-leading-segments
@itemx extra-leading-segments
Segment lists replacing glyph slots in that line's leading area.  A line
must not specify both leading segments and leading glyph slots.

@item leading-width
A minimum leading-area width in columns, or a function of one context
returning that width.  @code{nil} means zero.  Both lines share the
minimum, which an adapter can compute across a view for alignment.
@end table

  Any non-@code{nil} extra-line content field adds the second visual
line.  A layout is still one logical entry.  The option
@code{tessera-entry-layout} selects its name unless a thread context and
registered thread layout take precedence.

@node Glyph Definitions
@section Glyph Definitions

@deftp {Data Type} tessera-glyph
Alternative visual forms and a semantic role.  Construct with
@code{make-tessera-glyph}; fields are @code{ascii}, @code{unicode},
@code{nerd-icons}, and @code{semantic}.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-glyph
@findex tessera-glyph-p
@findex tessera-glyph-ascii
@findex tessera-glyph-unicode
@findex tessera-glyph-nerd-icons
@findex tessera-glyph-semantic
  The ASCII form must be a nonempty ASCII string.  The Unicode form must
be a nonempty string.  The Nerd Icons descriptor is a plist with
@code{:function} naming a non-@code{nil} symbol and @code{:name} holding
a nonempty string.  All three forms are supplied even when a particular
installation uses only one.  @code{semantic} must be a recognized role.

  For example:

@lisp
(make-tessera-glyph
 :ascii "*" :unicode "●"
 :nerd-icons '(:function nerd-icons-mdicon :name "nf-md-email")
 :semantic 'accent)
@end lisp

@defun tessera-glyph-render glyph context &optional properties
Return the rendered glyph string for the entry @var{context}, applying
style fallback, color, hover, and interaction properties.  Validate
@var{glyph}, @var{context}, and @var{properties}, signalling errors for
invalid input.
@end defun

  @var{properties} supports @code{:face}, @code{:mouse-face},
@code{:help-echo}, @code{:keymap}, @code{:pointer}, and
@code{:follow-link}.  @code{:face} must name an existing face and is
used only in semantic-color mode.  Do not pass @code{:glyph} here: the
glyph is already the first argument.  Standalone rendering does not
reserve a slot width.

@node Glyph Slots
@section Glyph Slots

@deftp {Data Type} tessera-glyph-slot
A fixed-width channel with one selected variant.  Construct with
@code{make-tessera-glyph-slot}; fields are @code{name}, @code{selector},
@code{width}, @code{align}, and @code{glyphs}.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-glyph-slot
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-p
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-name
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-selector
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-width
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-align
@findex tessera-glyph-slot-glyphs
  The name is a non-@code{nil} symbol unique within its backend.  The
selector receives an entry context and returns a variant symbol or
@code{nil}.  Width is a positive integer in columns; alignment is
@code{left}, @code{center}, or @code{right}.

  The @code{glyphs} field is an alist of unique variant symbols followed
by property lists.  Each variant requires @code{:glyph}, containing a
@code{tessera-glyph}; it can also have the interaction properties
accepted by @code{tessera-glyph-render}.  Its ASCII form must fit the
slot at registration time; selected visual forms are also checked when
rendered.

  A @code{nil} selection normally produces empty fixed-width space.  An
unknown variant produces a warning and an empty slot.  Keep selector
results within the registered variants.

  A layout can reference a slot by its symbol, or by a list containing
its name and boolean properties:

@lisp
(status :reserve t)
(attachment :optional t)
@end lisp

@noindent
@code{:reserve t} hides the selected glyph while reserving its display
space, which can align a second line with the first.  @code{:optional t}
omits a slot, including its width, when the selector returns @code{nil}.
An unknown variant still warns and reserves empty space.

  Within a segment list, @code{(:slots SLOT...)} places a nonempty group
of fixed-width slots inline with text.  The group behaves as one fixed
segment.  Prefix packing controlled by @code{glyph-slots-align} does not
pack these inline slots.

@node Rendering and Insertion
@section Rendering and Insertion
@cindex overlay attachment
  Rendering produces entry text; attaching its layout installs the
decorations in the destination buffer.  The adapter also maintains or
removes those decorations when native rows change.

@defun tessera-entry-render backend object &optional window prefix
Return one logical line of rendered content and its layout metadata for
@var{object}.  Use the registered @var{backend}.  @var{window} defaults
to a window displaying the current buffer; a supplied dead window is an
error.  The context provider runs with the current buffer as its buffer
argument.
@end defun

  @var{prefix}, when supplied, is native non-displaying text placed
before the content.  It is excluded from width allocation and anchors
layout outside content hover ranges.  The adapter is responsible for
supplying its non-displaying properties.  Without it, the engine inserts
a zero-width anchor.  Preserve the result's properties when inserting
it.

  When there is no display window, the engine cannot allocate fields to
a window width.  Render again for the intended window when establishing
a view.  Rendering does not insert a terminating newline or attach
buffer overlays.

@defun tessera-entry-apply-layout start end
Attach the rendered layout to content from @var{start} to @var{end} in
the current buffer.  @var{end} excludes the client's terminating
newline.  Insert that newline first when bottom padding is nonzero;
otherwise attachment signals an error.  Reapplying replaces only the
entry's Tessera layout overlays and preserves native content properties.
@end defun

  A typical insertion sequence is:

@lisp
(let ((start (point)))
  (insert (tessera-entry-render backend object window))
  (let ((end (point)))
    (insert "\n")
    (tessera-entry-apply-layout start end)))
@end lisp

@noindent
Here @code{backend}, @code{object}, and @code{window} are supplied by
the adapter.  This sequence must run with suitable write permission in
the destination buffer.

@defun tessera-entry-layout-applied-p start
Return non-@code{nil} when the current rendered layout at @var{start}
still has its associated live overlay.  Use this after native updates to
decide whether layout attachment must be restored.
@end defun

@defun tessera-entry-clear-layout &optional start end
Remove Tessera layout overlays within the supplied bounds of the current
buffer.  Omitted bounds select the accessible buffer.  Restore the
current-entry highlighting first if its range overlaps the cleanup.
@end defun

  Cleanup does not regenerate native rows.  Disabling an adapter must
also restore the print settings and native presentation that it owns.

@node Navigation Anchors
@section Navigation Anchors
@cindex navigation anchors
@defun tessera-entry-point &optional string
Return the first preferred navigation position in @var{string}, or in
the current logical line when @var{string} is omitted.  String positions
are zero-based indexes; buffer positions use ordinary Emacs positions.
Return @code{nil} when no displayed segment has an anchor.
@end defun

  A segment reference with @code{:point t} marks its first displayed
character.  This identifies meaningful message text independently of the
prefix, glyph capacity, and overlay spacing.

@defun tessera-entry-save-point
Return an opaque snapshot of point within its logical entry.  It
contains a marker that must later be released through restoration.
@end defun

@defun tessera-entry-restore-point snapshot
Restore point from @var{snapshot} in its original buffer when possible.
An anchored position follows the newly rendered anchor; other positions
retain their character offset, bounded by the new line end.  Release the
saved marker even if restoration cannot complete.
@end defun

  Use @code{unwind-protect} around a redraw so restoration and marker
release occur even when the redraw signals an error.

@node Highlight Lifecycle
@section Highlight Lifecycle

@defun tessera-entry-highlight-current
Highlight the entry at point in the current buffer, restoring the
previous entry first.  Preserve native faces and interactions and
exclude vertical padding and virtual thread headings.  This is suitable
for a buffer-local @code{post-command-hook}.
@end defun

@defun tessera-entry-clear-current
Restore the highlighted entry's original faces and saved decorations,
and release its boundary markers.  Do nothing when no highlight is
active in the current buffer.
@end defun

  The adapter is responsible for installing and removing the hook and
for avoiding a competing native whole-line overlay.  Neither function
moves point to another native record.

@node Backend Example
@section A Small Backend Example

  The following example registers a minimal list renderer with a title
and date.  All @samp{my-} names belong to the example.  It does not
install a native adapter or modify the built-in backend registrations.

@lisp
(require 'tessera)

(defun my-tessera-context (object buffer window)
  "Describe OBJECT in BUFFER and WINDOW."
  (make-tessera-entry-context
   :backend 'my-list :object object
   :buffer buffer :window window))

(defun my-tessera-title (context)
  "Return the title in CONTEXT."
  (plist-get (tessera-entry-context-object context) :title))

(defun my-tessera-date (context)
  "Return the date in CONTEXT."
  (plist-get (tessera-entry-context-object context) :date))

(tessera-entry-register
 'my-list
 :context #'my-tessera-context
 :segments '((title . my-tessera-title) (date . my-tessera-date))
 :layouts
 (list (cons 'single-line
             (make-tessera-entry-layout
              :main-left-segments
              '((title :grow t :min-width 4
                       :truncate tail :point t))
              :main-right-segments '(date)))))

(pop-to-buffer (get-buffer-create "*Tessera Example*"))
(let ((inhibit-read-only t)
      (tessera-entry-layout 'single-line))
  (erase-buffer)
  (let ((start (point)))
    (insert (tessera-entry-render
             'my-list '(:title "One record" :date "2026-09-17")
             (selected-window)))
    (let ((end (point)))
      (insert "\n")
      (tessera-entry-apply-layout start end))))
(goto-char (point-min))
(special-mode)
@end lisp

  A production adapter must additionally connect native navigation,
refresh, resizing, point preservation, and reversible activation.
Registration and one insertion alone do not provide those lifecycle
operations.

@node Thread Backend Interfaces
@chapter Thread Backend Interfaces

  These interfaces are for Lisp programmers adding thread display to an
adapter.  Supply the ordered messages and their reply relationships;
Tessera uses them to build headings, tree connectors, and spacing.
These functions do not discover missing messages.

@menu
* Thread Contexts: Building Thread Contexts.  Deriving counts, paths, and boundaries.
* Thread Layouts: Thread Layouts and Segments.  Rendering thread headings and branches.
@end menu

@node Building Thread Contexts
@section Building Thread Contexts

@defun tessera-thread-build-contexts entries
Return an @code{equal} hash table from native IDs to thread contexts.
Each ordered input row is @code{(ID PARENT UNREAD VISIBLE)}.
@code{PARENT} is @code{nil} for a displayed root or names an earlier
row; a missing preceding parent signals an error.  Use distinct IDs.
@end defun

  @code{UNREAD} and @code{VISIBLE} are boolean observations supplied by
the adapter.  Counts include all rows, including folded ones; the last
visible row determines the bottom boundary.  The adapter supplies any
visible representative and root adoption required by the native view.  A
native root absent from the results is not reconstructed here.

@deftp {Data Type} tessera-thread-context
The position of one member in a displayed native thread.  Construct with
@code{make-tessera-thread-context}, or obtain it from
@code{tessera-thread-build-contexts}.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-thread-context
@findex tessera-thread-context-p
@findex tessera-thread-context-id
@findex tessera-thread-context-parent
@findex tessera-thread-context-root
@findex tessera-thread-context-first
@findex tessera-thread-context-last
@findex tessera-thread-context-total
@findex tessera-thread-context-unread
  The constructor keywords are @code{:id}, @code{:parent}, @code{:root},
@code{:path}, @code{:first}, @code{:last}, @code{:total}, and
@code{:unread}.  IDs are opaque native identifiers.  @code{first}
identifies the displayed representative; @code{last} identifies the last
visible member.  @code{total} and @code{unread} count all members in the
result set, including folded records.

@defun tessera-thread-context-path context
Return the root-to-child branch path.  Each boolean describes whether
that branch has a following sibling; the representative itself has no
branch entry.  This is also a @code{setf} place for updating the path.
@end defun

  For example, @code{(t nil)} describes a child below an ancestor branch
that continues, with no following sibling at the child's own level.

@defun tessera-thread-context-key context
Return the fields affecting the displayed row, or @code{nil} for a
@code{nil} context.  The key includes boundaries and the branch path;
counts affect the representative only.  It can be compared with
@code{equal} to decide whether a native row needs redrawing.
@end defun

@node Thread Layouts and Segments
@section Thread Layouts and Segments

@deftp {Data Type} tessera-thread-layout
A pair of ordinary entry layouts, in fields @code{head} and
@code{child}.  Construct with @code{make-tessera-thread-layout} and
register it with @code{tessera-entry-register}'s @code{:thread-layout}
argument.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-thread-layout
@findex tessera-thread-layout-p
@findex tessera-thread-layout-head
@findex tessera-thread-layout-child
  When an entry context supplies thread membership, @code{first} selects
@code{head}; other members select @code{child}.  A @code{nil} thread
context selects the ordinary layout.  Both thread layouts are validated
with the backend's normal segments and slots.

@defun tessera-thread-count context
Return an unread/total string for the thread in the entry @var{context},
with default semantic styling and count help.  Return @code{nil} without
thread membership.
@end defun

@defun tessera-thread-prefix context
Return tree connectors for the native member described by entry
@var{context}, or @code{nil} when there is no branch path.  Respect
@code{tessera-glyph-style} for connector style and
@code{tessera-entry-segment-gap} for indentation.
@end defun

  These functions are segment providers.  Register the tree as fixed
content: shrinking or omitting it would misrepresent parentage.
Backend-specific providers may compose native faces over the returned
strings.  The adapter must also update the display when native folding
changes which messages are visible.

@node Native Adapter Interfaces
@chapter Native Adapter Interfaces

  The following public functions connect the built-in adapters to native
format or fold protocols.  Ordinary users should enable the mode rather
than installing these functions individually.

@menu
* Format Callbacks: Native Format Callbacks.  Connecting to Gnus and Elfeed formatters.
* Fold Observation: Native Fold Observation.  Tracking and padding mu4e folds.
@end menu

@node Native Format Callbacks
@section Native Format Callbacks

@defun tessera-gnus-summary-format-entry header
Return a Tessera representation of the Gnus mail @var{header} using the
native summary formatter's dynamically supplied mark and contact state.
Retain the four native mark characters and navigation metadata.  This
function is a format callback, not a standalone header formatter.
@end defun
@findex gnus-user-format-function-tessera
  @code{gnus-user-format-function-tessera} aliases that callback for
Gnus's @samp{%u&tessera;} format entry.  The adapter arranges the
required metadata, registration, and later overlay attachment.

@defun tessera-elfeed-search-print-entry entry
Insert a Tessera representation of the native Elfeed @var{entry} at
point in the current Search buffer.  If the native terminating newline
is already present, attach its layout immediately.  The update hook
otherwise attaches layouts after Elfeed inserts terminators.
@end defun

  The Elfeed mode installs this function in
@code{elfeed-search-print-entry-function}.  Calling it directly requires
an already registered backend and a writable insertion location.

@node Native Fold Observation
@section Native Fold Observation

  These mu4e functions follow native fold overlays in the current
buffer.  They do not implement an independent fold command or thread
switch.

@defun tessera-mu4e-thread-track enable
Install advice to observe native fold, unfold, and unfold-all operations
when @var{enable} is non-@code{nil}; remove that advice otherwise.  The
adapter manages this shared tracking while its views need it.
@end defun

@defvar tessera-mu4e-thread-change-hook
A buffer-local normal hook run after an observed native fold operation.
Its functions take no arguments.  The Headers adapter uses it to mark
its view for refresh.
@end defvar

@defun tessera-mu4e-thread-invalidate
Invalidate the current buffer's cached fold list after native edits.
@end defun

@defun tessera-mu4e-thread-folds
Return native fold overlays in buffer order, rebuilding the cache when
invalid.  Treat the list and overlays as native state, not as copies
owned by the caller.
@end defun

@defun tessera-mu4e-thread-fold-at position
Return a native folded overlay covering @var{position}, or @code{nil}.
@end defun

@defun tessera-mu4e-thread-pad-folds contexts
Replace Tessera's fold-padding overlays using the native docid-to-thread
hash table @var{contexts}.  Keep native folds and their summary text.
Use shared inner top and appropriate inner or outer bottom padding.
@end defun

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

  If the display differs from what you expect, check the active layout,
message state, and face settings described below.

@menu
* Layout Changes: Missing Layout Changes.  Checking layout and spacing settings.
* States and Faces: Unexpected State or Faces.  Checking marks, glyphs, and styling.
* Reporting Bugs::      Information to include in a bug report.
@end menu

@node Missing Layout Changes
@section Missing Layout Changes

  If changing @code{tessera-entry-layout} has no effect, check the
buffer's local value and whether native threading is active.  Activation
sets a local two-line default, and threading selects its own layout.
For Elfeed, force a Search update after changing shared options.

  If an entry appears to have incorrect spacing, check dimensions,
window width, and the font used by a glyph.  Fixed fields need room;
thread connectors deliberately retain native depth.  A missing glyph may
be a fallback or an absent eligible state rather than missing text.

@node Unexpected State or Faces
@section Unexpected State or Faces

  Compare a glyph with the native marks or flags and its hover help.
Several states can share one prefix channel.  mu4e also applies native
flag visibility; Gnus follows its native mark characters and highlight
rules.  Thread headings use aggregate unread state, while message dates
use per-message state.

  A content indicator does not certify a signature or decrypt a message.
An absent Gnus attachment icon can mean that the content has not yet
been observed.  Inspect the article through Gnus when native observation
is needed, rather than treating an unknown state as absence.

  After manual face changes, regenerate the native view if resolved
hover colors are stale.  Disable the corresponding Tessera mode when
comparing with the native presentation, then enable it again when
finished.

@node Reporting Bugs
@section Reporting Bugs

  Report reproducible problems at
@url{https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera/issues}.  Include Emacs,
Tessera, and backend versions; the active layout and native thread
setting; relevant options and theme; and the expected and observed
behavior.  For a display problem, include whether it occurs in a
graphical or terminal frame and whether resizing changes it.

  Prefer a small reproducer that demonstrates the state without private
mail or feed contents.  Experimental selection or retrieval problems
belong with the corresponding @samp{tessera-x-*} manual's instructions.

